{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775376810-pEmaCgj19JZUXKCGE22A","role":"assistant","created":1775376839,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the data I need. Let me extract the total surplus/deficit values for calendar years 1989-2013. I'll need to collect these values from the various Treasury Bulletin documents:\n\nFrom treasury_bulletin_1994_06_page_28.txt (1989-1993):\n- 1989: -152,087\n- 1990: -220,388\n- 1991: -268,729\n- 1992: -290,204\n- 1993: -254,948\n\nFrom treasury_bulletin_1999_06_page_15.txt (1994-1998):\n- 1994: -203,370\n- 1995: -163,813\n- 1996: -107,331\n- 1997: -22,618\n- 1998: 70,039\n\nFrom treasury_bulletin_2004_06_page_18.txt (1999-2003):\n- 1999: 125,974\n- 2000: 236,917\n- 2001: 127,401\n- 2002: -157,823\n- 2003: -374,791\n\nFrom treasury_bulletin_2009_06_page_20.txt (2004-2008):\n- 2004: -412,986\n- 2005: -318,298\n- 2006: -248,197\n- 2007: -161,527\n- 2008: -454,798\n\nFrom treasury_bulletin_2014_06_page_19.txt (2009-2013):\n- 2009: -1,415,722\n- 2010: -1,294,204\n- 2011: -1,295,591\n- 2012: -1,089,353\n\n\n- 2013: -680,276\n\nI also notice there's a 2014 estimate of -648,805 and a 2015 estimate of -563,564, though these won't be relevant for the 1989-2013 range. Now I'll compile the complete dataset spanning from 1989 through 2013, which includes both the surplus years in the late 1990s and early 2000s as well as the growing deficits leading up to the financial crisis.\n\nThe data shows a clear pattern: large deficits in the early 1990s, a brief period of surpluses from 1998-2001, then a return to deficits that escalated dramatically starting in 2008. I need to fit a cubic polynomial to this series and use it to project forward to 2025.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_cc5a3137cc764bd4bbb1a213","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Extract surplus/deficit data for calendar years 1989-2013\n# Data from the Treasury Bulletin pages\n\ndata = {\n    # From treasury_bulletin_1994_06_page_28.txt\n    1989: -152087,\n    1990: -220388,\n    1991: -268729,\n    1992: -290204,\n    1993: -254948,\n    # From treasury_bulletin_1999_06_page_15.txt\n    1994: -203370,\n    1995: -163813,\n    1996: -107331,\n    1997: -22618,\n    1998: 70039,\n    # From treasury_bulletin_2004_06_page_18.txt\n    1999: 125974,\n    2000: 236917,\n    2001: 127401,\n    2002: -157823,\n    2003: -374791,\n    # From treasury_bulletin_2009_06_page_20.txt\n    2004: -412986,\n    2005: -318298,\n    2006: -248197,\n    2007: -161527,\n    2008: -454798,\n    # From treasury_bulletin_2014_06_page_19.txt\n    2009: -1415722,\n    2010: -1294204,\n    2011: -1295591,\n    2012: -1089353,\n    2013: -680276,\n}\n\n# Filter to 1989-2013\nyears = list(range(1989, 2014))\nsurplus_deficit = [data[y] for y in years]\n\nprint(\"Calendar year data (1989-2013):\")\nfor y, v in zip(years, surplus_deficit):\n    print(f\"{y}: {v:,}\")\n\n# Fit cubic polynomial regression\nX = np.array(years)\ny = np.array(surplus_deficit)\n\n# Use polynomial fit\ncoeffs = np.polyfit(X, y, 3)\nprint(f\"\\nCubic polynomial coefficients:\")\nprint(f\"  a3: {coeffs[0]:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"  a2: {coeffs[1]:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"  a1: {coeffs[2]:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"  a0: {coeffs[3]:.6f}\")\n\n# Predict for 2025\npoly = np.poly1d(coeffs)\nprediction_2025 = poly(2025)\nprint(f\"\\nPrediction for 2025: {prediction_2025:,.2f} million dollars\")\n\n# Also check the fit quality for the data range\ny_pred = poly(X)\nss_res = np.sum((y - y_pred) ** 2)\nss_tot = np.sum((y - np.mean(y)) ** 2)\nr2 = 1 - (ss_res / ss_tot)\nprint(f\"R-squared: {r2:.4f}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
